<h2>Items Often Missed on San Antonio Estimates</h2>
<h3>Roofs</h3>
<p>Bexar County has logged more than 600 NOAA hail reports since 1957, with April and May the peak months, so roof estimates are some of the most common we review. Frequently missed items include drip edge, starter course, ridge and hip caps, pipe jacks and vents, step and counter flashing, valley materials, steep and high roof charges, and gutter damage. Standing-seam metal roofs on the Hill Country edge involve trim and closures that a shingle-focused scope may leave out. See <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> and our guide on <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">reviewing an underpaid roof estimate</a>.</p>
<h3>Exteriors</h3>
<p>Stucco, limestone, and brick veneer are common in San Antonio and often need specialized trades. Estimates may include only one elevation of siding or paint when matching requires more, or omit window screens, fence sections, and outdoor HVAC fins damaged by hail.</p>
<h3>Interiors</h3>
<p>For water and freeze losses, look for drying equipment days, insulation, baseboards, cabinetry, flooring transitions, and paint to a natural break. After Winter Storm Uri, many San Antonio homes had damage in several rooms from a single burst line. See <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Older homes</h3>
<p>Pier-and-beam homes in neighborhoods like Tobin Hill, King William, and Monte Vista can have plank decking, plaster, wood siding, and trim profiles that standard line items do not price correctly.</p>

