<h2>How Our Estimate Review Works</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Collect the documents.</strong> The insurer's complete estimate, summary page, photo report, payment letters, and your declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect the property.</strong> We photograph and measure the damage. Matterport 3D imaging can capture interior conditions and dimensions.</li>
<li><strong>Compare line by line.</strong> We check each item against what we found, flagging missing scope, short quantities, mismatched line items, and pricing questions.</li>
<li><strong>Explain the results.</strong> We walk you through what we found in plain English, including items where the insurer's estimate looks reasonable.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare our own estimate if warranted.</strong> If there are meaningful gaps, we prepare an Xactimate estimate in the same format so differences are easy to see.</li>
<li><strong>Next steps.</strong> Depending on the claim, that may mean a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a>, a request to reopen, or, where the dispute is only about amount, a discussion of <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a>.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Reading the Estimate Summary</h2>
<p>The last pages of an estimate usually show how the payment was calculated. Understanding these terms can explain why a check is smaller than the estimate total:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>RCV (replacement cost value):</strong> the estimated cost to repair or replace with like kind and quality.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation:</strong> a reduction for age and condition. On replacement cost policies, part or all may be recoverable after repairs.</li>
<li><strong>ACV (actual cash value):</strong> RCV minus depreciation.</li>
<li><strong>Deductible:</strong> often a percentage wind/hail deductible on Texas policies; check your declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>Net claim:</strong> what the insurer is paying now.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> lets you model these numbers, and our article on <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a> explains the differences.</p>

<h2>Common Estimate Terms and Abbreviations</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it usually means</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>SQ</td><td>Roofing square, equal to 100 square feet of roof surface</td></tr>
<tr><td>SF / LF / EA</td><td>Square feet, linear feet, and each; the unit a line item is priced in</td></tr>
<tr><td>R&amp;R</td><td>Remove and replace</td></tr>
<tr><td>D&amp;R</td><td>Detach and reset, such as removing and reinstalling a satellite dish or gutter guard</td></tr>
<tr><td>Waste</td><td>Extra material added to account for cutting, typically shown as a separate quantity on roofing</td></tr>
<tr><td>O&amp;P</td><td>General contractor overhead and profit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Price list</td><td>The regional pricing database and month the estimate was priced with</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If a line item or abbreviation on your estimate is not clear, ask the insurer's adjuster to explain it in writing. A clear explanation is part of understanding your claim.</p>

